[#:seon.task{:name "decoding-pays-a-projection-scope-and-cache-claim-per-value"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Each decoded attribute value enters `seon.schema/call-with-projection`, `seon.cache/claim` and compiled-storage separately. An agent's (`seon.db/q` '[:find (pull ?e [*]) :where [?e `:seon.cluster.eval/source` _]] db) over 715 evaluations took 1,529 ms and 937,544 armed calls: projection-cache-value 169,764, call-with-projection 47,309 (756 ms), compiled-storage 47,316 (518 ms), edn-encoded-attr-in? 47,309 (530 ms), `seon.cache/claim` 47,303 (default 2026-10-07). Over a 50 min session compiled-storage ran 551,856 times and allocated 1.85 GB self in one 60 s JFR window; projection-cache-value ran 3.94 M times. Fix: resolve the projection and each attribute's decoder once per query/pull (per result shape), then decode values with the resolved decoders. Done when the 715-entity pull answers in under 150 ms with armed calls proportional to attributes, not datoms; regression: the pull probe as a test with a call-count bound."
             :must-read #{seon.schema.datahike/compiled-storage seon.schema.datahike/decode-attribute-value-in seon.schema/call-with-projection}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--profile-delta-table.txt" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--jfr-large-errors-alloc.txt"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-attribute-query-calls-logical-attribute-once-per-datom"
  :kind :function
  :intent "A whole-database attribute query ([:find ?a (count ?e) :where [?e ?a] [(namespace ?a) ?n] [(contains? ..)]]) over an agent database took 2,153 ms and made 568,597 armed calls, 568,485 of them to `seon.schema.datahike/logical-attribute?` (datahike.clj:919-923 adds it as a predicate clause evaluated for every datom's attribute). Work is proportional to datoms, not to the distinct attributes the answer names. Correct: the predicate (or the attribute-shape check it implements) runs once per distinct attribute, or the clause is planned over the attribute index. Proof: the same query on a fixed database fixture asserts logical-attribute? calls <= distinct attributes, and the evaluation stays under one second."
  :must-read #{seon.schema.datahike/logical-attribute? seon.db/q}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/macro-design.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-ordinary-pass-builds-hundreds-of-storability-refusals"
             :kind :function
             :intent "One MCP SCI pass calling a contracted one-line function (pf 41) made 128,825 armed calls on default 2026-10-08: attribute-declaration 8,149, storable-attribute-in? 8,263 and 451 `seon.error.refusal/diagnostic`, about 35 ms. attribute-declaration answers an unstorable member with a full diagnostic (Date, world references, a message built with str and a printed registration form) as control flow, and nothing keeps the answer across passes for an unchanged projection. Correct: storability per attribute is a derived value cached by projection content; the refusal value is built only where a caller delivers it. Proof: a pass calling one contracted function makes zero `seon.error.refusal/diagnostic` calls and fewer than 100 attribute-declaration calls."
             :must-read #{seon.schema.datahike/attribute-declaration seon.schema.datahike/declared-storable?}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/review/redo-review-opus-redo-review.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-datahike-fork-collapses-distinct-incomparable-values"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Our Datahike fork's safe-compare (reference-code/datahike/src/datahike/datom.cljc:317-321) falls back to class-order, which answers 0 for two distinct values of the same class that compare cannot order, so the index silently keeps one: under :schema-flexibility :read with cardinality-many :t/m, transacting [[:db/add 101 :t/m [1 2]] [:db/add 101 :t/m [1 \"x\"]]] reports two added datoms and stores only [[1 2]]; retracting a stored vector holding a map throws ClassCastException. Seon's write-mode schema type-checks each slot, so no Seon attribute is known to reach it today; any opaque or :db.type/any value would. Fix in the fork at the producer: class-order never answers 0 for distinct values (break ties with a total EDN order, as `seon.schema.datahike/storage-compare` does, or a value hash), or validate-val refuses values compare cannot order. Done when a fork test stores both values and retracts the map-holding vector, and Seon's tests pass on the bumped gitlink."
             :must-read #{seon.schema.datahike/storage-compare seon.schema.datahike/compiled-storage}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/vectors/vectors.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-codec-reprints-values-to-compare-them"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Storing an EDN slot printed every keyword and symbol through `seon.id/printed` to check it reads back, sorted every map and set with `seon.print/compare-values`, and decoding printed the read value again to judge it canonical. An eval request whose call read 440,605 times recorded 440,605 read-evidence rows (8,266 distinct) and spent about 66 s in `seon.turn/receipt-settle-batch-call` encoding them (default 2026-10-08). Done when equal reads are one evidence row, an identifier's readability is decided once, maps and sets of one natural-order class sort by `compare`, and a read never prints. Left: `seon.schema.datahike/storage-compare` still falls back to printed text for two distinct same-class values `compare` cannot order; replacing it changes the stored order of such values and is the owner's storage decision."
             :must-read #{seon.schema.datahike/storage-compare seon.db/read-evidence seon.turn/receipt-settle-batch-call}}]
